      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14217628/assets/ca545b153cd04a539f30030c27a90dc4489b3a620e9a0d859bae5b531fcc7144.png)\n\nThe equation of the curve above is $y=\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}-3x+k.$ Find the value of $k.$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that if we substitute $x=0$ into the given equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}-3x+k \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{2}(0)^{2}-3(0)+k \\\\ y & =k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nmeaning that the curve intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,k).$ Based on the graph, we see that we must have $k=\\frac{1}{2}$ since our parabola intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,\\frac{1}{2}).$",
